"tsfresh.feature_extraction package — tsfresh 0.21.2.post0.dev2+g6af3c00 documentation (https://tsfresh.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api/tsfresh.feature_extraction.html)\nciteturn12view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://tsfresh.readthedocs.io/en/latest/api/tsfresh.feature_extraction.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2166\nL0: cite0†tsfresh L1: \nL2: [Input: Search docs] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: cite0†tsfresh L5: \nL6: # tsfresh.feature_extraction packagecite1† L7: \nL8: ## Submodulescite2† L9: ## tsfresh.feature_extraction.data modulecite3† L10: \nL11: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.DaskTsAdapter(df, column_id, column_kind=None, column_value=None, column_sort=None)cite4†[source] cite5† L12: \nL13: \nL14: Bases: cite6†`TsData` L15: \nL16: apply(f, meta, **kwargs)cite7†[source] cite8† L17: \nL18: \nL19: Apply the wrapped feature extraction function “f” onto the data. Before that, turn the data into the correct form of Timeseries instances usable the the feature extraction. After the call, turn it back into pandas dataframes for further processing.\nL20: pivot(results)cite9†[source] cite10† L21: \nL22: \nL23: The extract features function for dask returns a dataframe of [id, variable, value]. Turn this into a pivoted dataframe, where only the variables are the columns and the ids are the rows.\nL24: \nL25: Attention: this is highly non-optimized!\nL26: \nL27: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.LongTsFrameAdapter(df, column_id, column_kind, column_value=None, column_sort=None)cite11†[source] cite12† L28: \nL29: \nL30: Bases: cite13†`PartitionedTsData` L31: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.PartitionedTsData(df, column_id)cite14†[source] cite13† L32: \nL33: \nL34: Bases: `Iterable`[cite15†`Timeseries` ], `Sized`, cite6†`TsData` L35: \nL36: Special class of TsData, which can be partitioned. Derived classes should implement __iter__ and __len__.\nL37: \nL38: pivot(results)cite16†[source] cite17† L39: \nL40: \nL41: Helper function to turn an iterable of tuples with three entries into a dataframe.\nL42: The input `list_of_tuples` needs to be an iterable with tuples containing three entries: (a, b, c). Out of this, a pandas dataframe will be created with all a’s as index, all b’s as columns and all c’s as values.\nL43: \nL44: It basically does a pd.pivot(first entry, second entry, third entry), but optimized for non-pandas input (= python list of tuples).\nL45: \nL46: This function is called in the end of the extract_features call.\nL47: \nL48: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.Timeseries(id, kind, data)cite18†[source] cite15† L49: Bases: cite15†`Timeseries` L50: \nL51: Timeseries tuple used for feature extraction.\nL52: \nL53: Make sure kind is of type str to allow inference of feature settings in feature_extraction.settings.from_columns.\nL54: \nL55: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.TsDatacite19†[source] cite6† L56: \nL57: \nL58: Bases: `object`\nL59: \nL60: TsData provides access to time series data for internal usage.\nL61: Distributors will use this data class to apply functions on the data. All derived classes must either implement the apply method, which is used to apply the given function directly on the data or the __iter__ method, which can be used to get an iterator of Timeseries instances (which distributors can use to apply the function on). Other methods can be overwritten if a more efficient solution exists for the underlying data store.\nL62: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.TsDictAdapter(ts_dict, column_id, column_value, column_sort=None)cite20†[source] cite21† L63: \nL64: \nL65: Bases: cite13†`PartitionedTsData` L66: \nL67: class tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.WideTsFrameAdapter(df, column_id, column_sort=None, value_columns=None)cite22†[source] cite23† L68: \nL69: \nL70: Bases: cite13†`PartitionedTsData` L71: \nL72: tsfresh.feature_extraction.data.to_tsdata(df, column_id=None, column_kind=None, column_value=None, column_sort=None)cite24†[source] cite25† L73: Wrap supported data formats as a TsData object, i.e. an iterable of individual time series.\nL74: \nL75: E.g. the DataFrame\nL76: \nL77: > id  | kind  | val\nL78: > --- | --- | ---\nL79: > 1  | a  | -0.21761\nL80: > 1  | a  | -0.613667\nL81: > 1  | a  | -2.07339\nL82: > 2  | b  | -0.576254\nL83: > 2  | b  | -1.21924\nL84: \nL85: into\nL86: \nL87: > Iterable((1, ‘a’, pd.Series([-0.217610, -0.613667, -2.073386]),\nL88: >\nL89: >\nL90: > (2, ‘b’, pd.Series([-0.576254, -1.219238]))\nL91: \nL92: Parameters:\nL93: \nL94: \nL95:   * df (pd.DataFrame|dict|cite6†TsData ) – one of the supported input formats\nL96:   * column_id (str|None) – The name of the id column to group by.\nL97: \nL98:   * column_kind (str|None) – The name of the column keeping record on the kind of the value.\nL99: \nL100:   * column_value (str|None) – The name for the column keeping the value itself.\nL101: \nL102:   * column_sort (str|None) – The name for the column to sort on.\nL103: \nL104: Returns:\nL105: \nL106: \nL107: a data adapter\nL108: \nL109: Return type:\nL110: \nL111: \nL112: cite6†TsData L113: ## tsfresh.feature_extraction.extraction modulecite26† L114: \nL115: This module contains the main function to interact with tsfresh: extract features\nL116: tsfresh.feature_extraction.extraction.extract_features(timeseries_container, default_fc_parameters=None, kind_to_fc_parameters=None, column_id=None, column_sort=None, column_kind=None, column_value=None, chunksize=None, n_jobs=1, show_warnings=False, disable_progressbar=False, impute_function=None, profile=False, profiling_filename='profile.txt', profiling_sorting='cumulative', distributor=None, pivot=True)cite27†[source] cite28† L117: \nL118: \nL119: Extract features from\nL120:   * a `pandas.DataFrame` containing the different time series\nL121: \nL122: or\nL123: \nL124:   * a dictionary of `pandas.DataFrame` each containing one type of time series\nL125: \nL126: In both cases a `pandas.DataFrame` with the calculated features will be returned.\nL127: \nL128: For a list of all the calculated time series features, please see the cite29†`ComprehensiveFCParameters` class, which is used to control which features with which parameters are calculated.\nL129: For a detailed explanation of the different parameters (e.g. the columns) and data formats please see cite30†Data Formats .\nL130: \nL131: Examples\nL132: \nL133:     >>> from tsfresh.examples import load_robot_execution_failures\nL134:     >>> from tsfresh import extract_features\nL135:     >>> df, _ = load_robot_execution_failures()\nL136:     >>> X = extract_features(df, column_id='id', column_sort='time')\nL137: \nL138: \nL139: Parameters:\nL140:   * timeseries_container (pandas.DataFrame or dict) – The pandas.DataFrame with the time series to compute the features for, or a dictionary of pandas.DataFrames.\nL141: \nL142:   * default_fc_parameters (dict) – mapping from feature calculator names to parameters. Only those names which are keys in this dict will be calculated. See the class:ComprehensiveFCParameters for more information.\nL143:   * kind_to_fc_parameters (dict) – mapping from kind names to objects of the same type as the ones for default_fc_parameters. If you put a kind as a key here, the fc_parameters object (which is the value), will be used instead of the default_fc_parameters. This means that kinds, for which kind_of_fc_parameters doe not have any entries, will be ignored by the feature selection.\nL144: \nL145:   * column_id (str) – The name of the id column to group by. Please see cite30†Data Formats .\nL146:   * column_sort (str) – The name of the sort column. Please see cite30†Data Formats .\nL147: \nL148:   * column_kind (str) – The name of the column keeping record on the kind of the value. Please see cite30†Data Formats .\nL149: \nL150:   * column_value (str) – The name for the column keeping the value itself. Please see cite30†Data Formats .\nL151: \nL152:   * n_jobs (int) – The number of processes to use for parallelization. If zero, no parallelization is used.\nL153:   * chunksize (None or int) – The size of one chunk that is submitted to the worker process for the parallelisation. Where one chunk is defined as a singular time series for one id and one kind. If you set the chunksize to 10, then it means that one task is to calculate all features for 10 time series. If it is set it to None, depending on distributor, heuristics are used to find the optimal chunksize. If you get out of memory exceptions, you can try it with the dask distributor and a smaller chunksize.\nL154:   * show_warnings (bool) – Show warnings during the feature extraction (needed for debugging of calculators).\nL155: \nL156:   * disable_progressbar (bool) – Do not show a progressbar while doing the calculation.\nL157: \nL158:   * impute_function (None or callable) – None, if no imputing should happen or the function to call for imputing the result dataframe. Imputing will never happen on the input data.\nL159: \nL160:   * profile (bool) – Turn on profiling during feature extraction\nL161:   * profiling_sorting (basestring) – How to sort the profiling results (see the documentation of the profiling package for more information)\nL162: \nL163:   * profiling_filename (basestring) – Where to save the profiling results.\nL164: \nL165:   * distributor (class) – Advanced parameter: set this to a class name that you want to use as a distributor. See the utilities/distribution.py for more information. Leave to None, if you want TSFresh to choose the best distributor.\nL166: \nL167: Returns:\nL168: The (maybe imputed) DataFrame containing extracted features.\nL169: \nL170: Return type:\nL171: \nL172: \nL173: pandas.DataFrame\nL174: ## tsfresh.feature_extraction.feature_calculators modulecite31† L175: \nL176: This module contains the feature calculators that take time series as input and calculate the values of the feature. There are two types of features:\nL177: \nL178:   1. feature calculators which calculate a single number (simple)\nL179: \nL180:   2. feature calculators which calculate a bunch of features for a list of parameters at once, to use e.g. cached results (combiner). They return a list of (key, value) pairs for each input parameter.\nL181: They are specified using the “fctype” parameter of each feature calculator, which is added using the set_property function. Only functions in this python module, which have a parameter called “fctype” are seen by tsfresh as a feature calculator. Others will not be calculated.\nL182: \nL183: Feature calculators of type combiner should return the concatenated parameters sorted alphabetically ascending.\nL184: \nL185: tsfresh.feature_extraction.feature_calculators.abs_energy(x)cite32†[source] cite33† L186: Returns the absolute energy of the time series which is the sum over the squared values\nL187: \nL188: cite34†Image: E = \\sum_{i=1,\\ldots, n} x_i^2 L189: \nL190: Parameters:\nL191: \nL192: \nL193: x (numpy.ndarray) – the time series to calculate the feature of\nL194: \nL195: Returns:\nL196: \nL197: \nL198: the value of this feature\nL199: \nL200: Return type:\nL201: \nL202: \nL203: float\nL204: \nL205: This function is of type: simple\nL206: \nL207: tsfresh.feature_extraction.feature_calculators.absolute_maximum(x)cite35†[source] cite36† L208: \nL209: \nL210: Calculates the highest absolute value of the time series x.\nL211: \nL212: Parameters:\nL213: x (numpy.ndarray) – the time series to calculate the feature of\nL214: \nL215: Returns:\nL216: \nL217: \nL218: the value of this feature\nL219: \nL220: Return type:\nL221: \nL222: \nL223: float\nL224: \nL225: This function is of type: simple\nL226: \nL227: tsfresh.feature_extraction.feature_calculators.absolute_sum_of_changes(x)cite37†[source] cite38† L228: \nL229: \nL230: Returns the sum over the absolute value of consecutive changes in the series x\nL231: \nL232: cite39†Image: \\sum_{i=1, \\ldots, n-1} \\mid x_{i+1}- x_i \\mid L233: \nL234: Parameters:\nL235: \nL236: \nL237: x (numpy.ndarray) – the time series to calculate the feature of\nL238: \nL239: Returns:\nL240: the value of this feature\nL241: \nL242: Return type:\nL243: \nL244: \nL245: float\nL246: \nL247: This function is of type: simple\nL248: \nL249: tsfresh.feature_extraction.feature_calculators.agg_autocorrelation(x, param)cite40†[source] cite41† L250: \nL251: \nL252: Descriptive statistics on the autocorrelation of the time series.\nL253: \nL254: Calculates the value of an aggregation function cite42†Image: f_{agg} (e.g. the variance or the mean) over the autocorrelation cite43†Image: R(l) for different lags. The autocorrelation cite43†Image: R(l) for lag cite44†Image: l is defined as\nL255: cite45†Image: R(l) = \\frac{1}{(n-l)\\sigma^2} \\sum_{t=1}^{n-l}(X_{t}-\\mu )(X_{t+l}-\\mu) L256: \nL257: where cite46†Image: X_i are the values of the time series, cite47†Image: n its length. Finally, cite48†Image: \\sigma^2 and cite49†Image: \\mu are estimators for its variance and mean (See cite50†Estimation of the Autocorrelation function†en.wikipedia.org ).\nL258: \nL259: The cite43†Image: R(l) for different lags cite44†Image: l form a vector. This feature calculator applies the aggregation function cite42†Image: f_{agg} to this vector and returns\nL260: cite51†Image: f_{agg} \\left( R(1), \\ldots, R(m)\\right) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad m = max(n, maxlag). L261: \nL262: Here cite52†Image: maxlag is the second parameter passed to this function.\nL263: \nL264: Parameters:\nL265: \nL266: \nL267:   * x (numpy.ndarray) – the time series to calculate the feature of\nL268:   * param (list) – contains dictionaries {“f_agg”: x, “maxlag”, n} with x str, the name of a numpy function (e.g. “mean”, “var”, “std”, “median”), its the name of the aggregator function that is applied to the autocorrelations. Further, n is an int and the maximal number of lags to consider.\nL269: \nL270: Returns:\nL271: \nL272: \n"
